  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Introduction</div>
    <p>Electrostatic shielding is a technique used to protect a region from external electrostatic fields. It's based on the principle that a conductor in electrostatic equilibrium has no electric field inside it. This means that any electric field outside the conductor will not penetrate the interior.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Definition</div>
    <p>Electrostatic shielding is the phenomenon where a conductive enclosure blocks external electrostatic fields from affecting the space inside.  Conversely, it also prevents electric fields generated inside the enclosure from affecting the outside environment.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Key Concepts</div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Conductors:</b> Materials that allow electric charge to move freely.</li>
      <li><b>Electrostatic Equilibrium:</b> A state where the charges within a conductor are at rest, and there is no net flow of charge.</li>
      <li><b>Gauss's Law:</b> A fundamental law of electromagnetism used to understand the behavior of electric fields.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
